"07_dialogs.mst — the MODAL LOOP, on this port's terms (DD12).

 Dolphin runs a modal dialog by FORKING A GREEN UI PROCESS
 (`DialogView>>runModalLoop`: `Processor forkMainIfMain`, priorities, a
 Semaphore to wait on) — process machinery this port deliberately replaced
 with Dart isolates and does not intend to imitate. The user's standing rule
 decides what to do instead: 'we are running the dolphin MVP but we are not
 dolphin... it is the GUI behaviour we need.'

 THE GUI BEHAVIOUR of a modal is: the owner is disabled, the dialog answers
 input, everything else keeps painting, and control returns to the caller when
 the dialog closes. That is exactly a NESTED MESSAGE LOOP in the current
 process — which is also what a native Win32 DialogBox does, and which
 Dolphin itself offers as `runModalInProcessLoop`:

     self isModal: true.
     SessionManager current inputState loopWhile: [self isModal]

 So the compat surface is two small pieces: `InputState>>loopWhile:` over the
 provisional pump, and `runModalLoop` routed to the in-process variant.
 STACKED modals fall out for free — each `showModal` nests one more
 `loopWhile:`, and they unwind innermost-first, which is the only order a
 single-threaded pump can unwind in and the order the goal gate asserts."

InputState extend [
    "Pump until aBlock answers false. Sleeps when the queue is empty for the
     same reason `UiSession runFor:` does: a tight PeekMessage loop on an
     empty queue burns a core. Deferred actions drain inside `pump`, which is
     what lets a gate drive a modal it is itself blocked under: the action it
     posted BEFORE entering `showModal` runs from the nested loop."
    loopWhile: aBlock [
        [ aBlock value ] whileTrue: [
            UiSession pump.
            KernelLibrary sleepEx: 5 bAlertable: false ]
    ]
]

DialogView extend [
    "In-process IS this port's process model, so the fork-a-UI-process loop
     routes to Dolphin's own in-process variant rather than reimplementing
     it. `isModal:` false is set by the dialog's own close path, which is what
     ends the loop."
    runModalLoop [
        ^self runModalInProcessLoop
    ]
]
